{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on R question `61031979`: yes, keep the minimal model.\n\n1. A slot is a tool and a named day. `day` is opaque text of at most\n   ten characters. The definition gives it no calendar meaning, no\n   timezone and no interval.\n2. The lapse time is the explicit field `until`. The definition\n   derives no day end. The member's instance supplies both values.\n3. State the boundary in the Lending README and in the implementation\n   note: two spellings of one calendar day are two slots, and the\n   scope does not check that `until` falls on the named day.\n4. The page and the agent instructions write the day in one form,\n   `YYYY-MM-DD`, so that two members name the same slot. That is a\n   convention of the application, not a guard.\n\nReason: the scenario tests slot exclusion, a refusal that no instance\npredicts, and the lapse of a member's own item. A calendar adds a\ntimezone policy and tests none of those. A canonical day can come\nlater as a field type, through its own request.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}